From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Maicon Alves <alvesmaiconn@gmail.com>
Date: Sat, 17 Oct 2026 01:54:12 -0300
Subject: [PATCH 27/27] client: the loading screen can wait for the terrain
 around the player

In singleplayer the loading screen ended once the textures and nodes were ready, and only then did
the server place the player and send the map: the world showed building itself around them, which
looks unfinished. With loading_terrain_radius (nodes, 0 = as before) the screen stays, as "Loading
terrain...", until the server has sent, culled or left out as air every block within that radius
(its RemoteClient's nearest unsent distance, read through the local server) and the client has made
every mesh asked for, or loading_terrain_timeout seconds pass. The player's pitch and yaw now come
with TOCLIENT_MOVE_PLAYER into the local player, so the position sent meanwhile asks for the blocks
the player will see first, and the camera's wanted range is set before the first frame. The time
taken goes to the log ("Terrain within N nodes loaded after T s").

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>

diff --git a/builtin/settingtypes.txt b/builtin/settingtypes.txt
index 13a52cb44..c10d9a725 100644
--- a/builtin/settingtypes.txt
+++ b/builtin/settingtypes.txt
@@ -498,6 +498,14 @@ fps_max_unfocused (FPS when unfocused) int 10 1 4294967295
 #    View distance in nodes.
 viewing_range (Viewing range) int 190 20 4000
 
+#    In singleplayer, the loading screen stays until the terrain this many nodes
+#    around where the player comes in is loaded and drawn, so the world doesn't
+#    show building itself. 0 enters at once, as before.
+loading_terrain_radius (Terrain loaded before entering) int 0 0 320
+
+#    The longest the loading screen waits for that terrain, in seconds.
+loading_terrain_timeout (Terrain loading timeout) float 30 1 300
+
 #    Undersampling is similar to using a lower screen resolution, but it applies
 #    to the game world only, keeping the GUI intact.
 #    It should give a significant performance boost at the cost of less detailed image.
